+## 7. MVP threat model
+
+### 7.1 Trusted components
+
+- The local desktop node, its Ed25519 identity key, its Git binary, and its local
+  mirror storage.
+- The repository owner/source node for source-of-truth repo-state attestations.
+- The mainnode for availability, discovery, routing, and metadata persistence,
+  but **not** for Git history correctness.
+
+### 7.2 Mainnode can do
+
+A malicious or broken mainnode can:
+
+- Observe metadata: account names, repo names, catalog records, timing,
+  connection metadata, IP-level information, presence, and bounty metadata.
+- Censor, drop, delay, reorder, rate-limit, or lose catalog, inbox, room, and Git
+  tunnel traffic.
+- Serve stale public catalog data until clients detect fresher signed records.
+- Deny service to hosts, mirrors, or clients.
+- Decrypt data it legitimately has keys for at runtime, including public
+  catalog/account data.
+
+### 7.3 Mainnode should not be able to do
+
+Assuming Ed25519 verification, canonical-string byte compatibility, and
+uncompromised local keys, the mainnode should not be able to:
+
+- Forge signed profile, catalog, issue, pull-request, release, host, view, or
+  push records.
+- Make a mirror serve a different public ref state when a valid source-attested
+  repo-state pin exists.
+- Become the canonical source of Git history, because Git objects are served from
+  desktop nodes and node-local mirrors.
+
+### 7.4 Known non-goals / gaps
+
+- Default shared room chat is not a strong secrecy boundary because the app key
+  is public in clients.
+- Token replay defense is timestamp freshness only; there is no server-side nonce
+  state.
+- Legacy repos without source attestations may fail open to mirror pins.
+- Push is experimental and owner-only in this protocol version.
+- Solana custody/payout paths need legal and third-party security review.
+- Multi-mainnode federation and cross-mainnode repo identity are roadmap work.
